      "title": "Note: Usage",
      "properties": {
        "widget_ue_connectable": {}
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "## Size\nQwen Image 2.1 has native 2K (2048*2048) support for direct output.\n\nOutput size comes from ResolutionSelector (width/height). Default is 1 megapixel (1024*1024). For native 2K, set 1:1 and 4 megapixels (2048*2048). Prefer multiples of 32.\n\n## Transparent Image\n\nTo generate a transparent image, wrap the prompt like this and replace the middle with the subject:\n\n---\nThis is an RGBA format image with transparency. `[your description]`. The image has an alpha channel and a transparent background.\n\n---\nSave as PNG to keep the alpha channel.\n\n## Parameters\n\n- prompt: what to generate. For RGBA, see Note: transparent.\n- negative_prompt: unused while cfg is 1.\n- cfg: keep 1 for the Qwen Image 2.1 official path. Raise it only if you use a negative prompt.\n- steps: Qwen Image 2.1 official  pipeline uses about 40-50 with euler. This template starts at 25. More advanced samplers need fewer steps.\n"
      ],
      "color": "#222",
      "bgcolor": "#000"
    },
    {
      "id": 486,
      "type": "Note",
      "pos": [
        341.5431823730469,
        2205.65087890625
      ],
      "size": [
        494.9602355957031,
        398.3733825683594
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 7,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {
        "widget_ue_connectable": {}
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "Use <image1> as the reference for the character’s identity, face, hairstyle, clothing, and accessories. Follow <image2> strictly as the template for the four-column layout, poses, and crops. The far-left panel must be a noticeably enlarged, front-facing head close-up: the head is upright, both eyes look directly at the camera, the entire hairstyle remains visible at the top, and the image is cropped at the upper chest. The face and hair occupy most of this panel; the waist, skirt, hands, and legs are outside the crop. The second panel must show a standing full-body front view, with the face and torso facing the camera, arms resting naturally, and both feet fully visible. The third panel shows a standard full-body side view facing the right edge of the frame. The fourth panel shows a standing full-body back view. Match the position, direction, figure scale, pose, and foot baseline of each corresponding panel in <image2>. The second panel must not become a side view or a half-body view, and the third panel must not be replaced by an additional side view facing the opposite direction. All four panels show the same person from <image1> with consistent identity and clothing details. Take only the poses and composition from <image2>, not the mannequin’s appearance. Plain white studio background, soft even lighting, no text or additional people.\n\n\nCreate a four-panel character sheet of the same person. Use <image1> only for the character’s identity, face, skin tone, body proportions, and hairstyle; use <image2> strictly for the four-panel layout, poses, viewing angles, figure scale, and crops; and use <image3> only as the clothing reference. Dress the person from <image1> in the outfit from <image3>, accurately preserving its design, colors, fabrics, patterns, cut, and visible decorative details. Do not retain the original clothing from <image1> or transfer any person’s identity, pose, or background from <image3>. The far-left panel is an enlarged front-facing head close-up: the head is upright, both eyes look directly at the camera, the complete hairstyle is visible, and the frame ends at the upper chest, showing only the neckline and shoulders of the outfit from <image3>; the waist, hands, and legs are outside this crop. The second panel is a standing full-body front view with the face and torso directed toward the camera. The third panel is a standard full-body side view facing the right edge of the frame. The fourth panel is a standing full-body back view. Match each corresponding figure in <image2> in head direction, shoulder angle, arm position, leg posture, figure scale, placement, and foot baseline. The second panel must remain a front view rather than becoming another side view. Keep the character’s identity, hairstyle, and outfit consistent across all four panels. The side and back of the outfit correspond coherently to the design visible in <image3>, with any unseen areas completed simply and plausibly. Use the plain white background and soft, even studio lighting of <image2>, taking only its poses and layout rather than the mannequins’ appearance. Include only these four views, with no text or additional people.\n"
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
